===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|en|id=remark|:inh|enm:note<id:remark>}}
From {{inh|en|enm|note<id:remark>}}, from {{inh|en|ang|not}}, {{m|ang|nōt||note, mark, sign}} and {{der|en|fro|note||letter, note}}, both from {{der|en|la|nota||mark, sign, remark, note}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# ''A [[symbol]] or [[annotation]].''
## A [[mark]] or [[token]] by which a thing may be known; a visible sign; a [[character]]; a distinctive mark or feature; a characteristic quality.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1594|author=w:Richard Hooker|title=Of the Lawes of Ecclesiastical Politie|section=book III|location=London|publisher=[[w:William Stansby|William Stansbye]]|year_published=1622|page=89|pageurl=https://archive.org/stream/gri_ofthelawesof00hook#page/n148|passage=As therefore they that are of the Myſticall Body of Chriſt, haue thoſe inward Graces and Vertues, whereby they differ from all others which are not of the ſame Body ; againe, whoſoeuer appertaine to the Viſible Body of the Church, they haue alſo the '''notes''' of externall Profeſſion, whereby the World knoweth what they are.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1841|author=w:John Henry Newman|title=A Letter to the Right Reverend Father in God, Richard, Lord Bishop of Oxford, on Occasion of No. 90, in the Series Called ''[[w:Tracts for the Times|The Tracts for the Times]]''|location=Oxford|publisher=w:John Henry Parker|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=lcsUAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA39|page=39
|passage=She [the Anglican church] has the '''Note''' of possession, the '''Note''' of freedom from party-titles ; the '''Note''' of life, a tough life and a vigorous ; she has ancient descent, unbroken continuance, agreement in doctrine with the ancient Church.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1888|author=w:Mary Augusta Ward|title=w:Robert Elsmere|volume=I|location=London|publisher=w:Macmillan and Co.|page=217|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=dG41AAAAMAAJ&pg=PA217|passage=What a '''note''' of youth, of imagination, of impulsive eagerness, there was through it all !}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1962|month=October|author=Brian Haresnape|title=Focus on B.R. passenger stations|journal=Modern Railways|page=251|text=For the first ten years of nationalisation a further '''note''' of overall gloom was added by the depressing policy of unimaginative Regional colour schemes, indifferently applied.}}
##* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|chapter=20|passage=The story struck the depressingly familiar '''note''' with which true stories ring in the tried ears of experienced policemen. No one queried it. It was in the classic pattern of human weakness, mean and embarrassing and sad.}}
## A mark, or [[sign]], made to call attention, to point out something to notice, or the like; a sign, or token, proving or giving evidence.
## A brief [[remark]]; a marginal comment or explanation; hence, an [[annotation]] on a text or author; a [[comment]]; a critical, explanatory, or illustrative observation.
# ''A written or printed communication or commitment.''
## A brief piece of writing intended to assist the memory; a [[memorandum]]; a [[minute]].
##: {{ux|en|I left him a '''note''' to remind him to take out the trash.}}
## A short informal [[letter]]; a [[billet]].
##* {{quote-video game|en|developer=w:BioWare|title={{w|Mass Effect: Andromeda}}|genre=[[science fiction|Science Fiction]]|location=Redwood City|publisher=Electronic Arts|year=2017|system=PC|scene=Ark Hyperion|oclc=1261299044|text=Garson: Drop me a '''note''' sometime. I'd love to hear how we're doing.}}
## {{lb|en|academic}} An academic [[treatise]] (often without regard to length); a [[treatment]]; a [[discussion]] [[paper]]; (loosely) any contribution to an academic [[discourse]].
## A diplomatic [[missive]] or written communication.
## {{senseid|en|document promising payment}} {{lb|en|finance}} A written or printed paper (or digital equivalent) acknowledging a [[debt#Noun|debt]], and promising [[payment#Noun|payment]].
##: {{nearsyn|en|draft<id:order for payment>|bond<id:Q11693>|paper<id:assets>|commercial paper#Noun|IOU#Noun|letter of credit#Noun}}
##: {{ux|en|a [[promissory note|promissory '''note''']]}}
##: {{ux|en|a '''note''' of hand}}
##: {{ux|en|a negotiable '''note'''}}
##* {{quote-song|en|year=1974|author={{w|Shirley Brown}}|title={{w|Woman to Woman (Shirley Brown song)|Woman to Woman}}|album=Woman to Woman|text=The car he drives / I pay the '''note''' every month}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} A list of items or of charges; an account.
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 4-2|act=V|scene=i|passage=Heere is now the Smithes '''note,''' for Shooing, / And Plough-Irons.}}
## A piece of paper or polymer [[money]]; a [[banknote]].
##: {{syn|en|bill}}
##: {{ux|en|I didn't have any coins to pay with, so I used a '''note'''.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|journal=w:National Geographic|year=1990|month=August|volume=178|number=2|title=Yugoslavia: A House Much Divided|author=Kenneth C. Danforth|page=110
|text=Seven months ago Prime Minister Ante Marković, a Croat, attacked Yugoslavia's 2,600 percent inflation rate by making the dinar convertible to Western currencies and lopping off four zeroes. The million-dinar '''note''' shrank to a hundred dinars.}}
## {{lb|en|extension}} A small size of paper used for writing letters or notes.
# {{lb|en|music}} ''A sound.''
## {{senseid|en|Q263478}}A character, variously formed, to indicate the length of a tone, and variously placed upon the staff to indicate its pitch.
## A musical sound; a [[tone]]; an utterance; a tune; a [[beat]] of a [[drum]].
##* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=III|lines=37–40|passage=Then feed on thoughts, that voluntarie move / Harmonious numbers ; as the wakeful Bird / Sings darkling, and in ſhadieſt Covert hid / Tunes her nocturnal '''Note'''.|year=1873}}
##* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|IV|47|Judge Short had gone to town, and Farrar was off for a three days' cruise up the lake. I was bitterly regretting I had not gone with him when the distant '''notes''' of a coach horn reached my ear, and I descried a four-in-hand winding its way up the inn road from the direction of Mohair.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1922|author=w:Michael Arlen|title=[http://openlibrary.org/works/OL1519647W “Piracy”: A Romantic Chronicle of These Days]|chapter=Ep./4/2
|passage=As they turned into Hertford Street they startled a robin from the poet's head on a barren fountain, and he fled away with a cameo '''note'''.}}
##* {{RQ:Bramah Eyes of Max Carrados|passage="That's Fred," said Mrs Fitzwilliam as the iron gate beyond the little plot of beaten earth that had once been a garden gave its individual '''note'''.}}
## {{lb|en|by extension}} A [[key]] of the piano or organ.
## {{lb|en|by extension}} A call or song of a bird.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1886|translator=H.L. Br&aelig;kstad|author=w:Peter Christen Asbjørnsen|title=Folk and Fairy Tales|page=85|passage=We heard the peculiar '''note''' of the woodcock, which resembles the repeated croaking of the frog, followed by a sharp hissing sound, somewhat like the noisy chirping of the wagtail[.]}}
## {{lb|en|rhythm games}} An indicator to which the [[player]] must correctly time some [[input]].
# {{lb|en|perfumery}} An element of a scent, fragrance, or perfume, especially as a descriptor or category.
#: {{hypo|en|top note|middle note|heart note|base note}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=w:National Geographic|year=1987|month=October|volume=172|number=4|title=Results of the Smell Survey|author=Avery N. Gilbert and Charles J. Wysocki|page=216
|text=Isoamyl acetate (''banana''): This familiar scent is known to most noses as the primary odor '''note''' in bananas and pears.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} [[observation|Observation]]; [[notice]]; [[heed]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice|V|i|passage=Go in ''Nerriſſa,'' / Giue order to my ſeruants, that they take / No '''note''' at all of our being abſent hence, / Nor you ''Lorenzo, Ieſſica'' nor you.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1625|author=w:Francis Bacon|chapter=Of ceremonies and reſpects|title=The Works of Francis Bacon|volume=III|location=London|publisher=J. and J. Knapton et al.|year_published=1730|page=373|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=VHNUAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA373|passage=So it is true, that ſmall matters win great commendation, becauſe they are continually in uſe, and in '''note''' ; whereas the occaſion of any great virtue cometh but on feſtivals.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} [[reputation|Reputation]]; [[distinction]].
#: {{ux|en|a poet of '''note'''}}
# A [[critical]] [[comment]].
#: {{ux|en|Your performance was fantastic! I have just one '''note''': you were a little flat in bars 35 and 36.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[notification|Notification]]; [[information]]; [[intelligence]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Mark of [[disgrace]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Lucrece|passage=That my poſteritie ſham’d with the '''note''' / Shall curſe my bones, and hold it for no ſinne, / To wiſh that I their father had not beene.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 2|I|i|23|2|Once more, the more to aggrauate the '''note''',<br>With a foule Traitors name ſtuffe I thy throte,<br>And wiſh (ſo pleaſe my Soueraigne) ere I moue,<br>What my tong ſpeaks, my right drawn ſword may proue}}

